  "slug": "single-nucleotide-precision-67f0",
  "summary": "Plasma cfDNA sequencing and refined mutational-signature methods now probe somatic change in tissues without direct biopsy.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is inferring somatic mutation burden and mutational processes in tissues that are not directly profiled. Two cfDNA preprints report that ultra-sensitive plasma sequencing detects clonal mosaicism in chronic liver disease, with ulcerative colitis also covered for mosaicism. The liver study reports liver-derived somatic mutations with etiology-linked patterns. Systemic sclerosis skin shows widespread somatic mosaicism attributed to ongoing oxidative DNA damage, with C>A burden tracking dcSSc fibrosis severity. The multiple myeloma study extends the repertoire of mutational processes through InDel signatures across 887 whole genomes, identifying a TXNDC5 hotspot and links to treatment outcome. The SEQUENCE method addresses sequence-context bias in signature extraction. The set is heterogeneous: the myeloma and SEQUENCE work concern mutational process inference, while the cfDNA and skin studies concern somatic detection in non-malignant or chronic tissue.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
